    {
      "question": "e-3129",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "35390a7ad9e7029854b733cb75ac22afb2d1f94e07fab15187d848019cb584ef",
      "new_sha256": "4ea4deb5309b990325ed2d47d380139b3c0cbe0de567934b5c2db3ef7c3ea1c7",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-3129",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "To find $f_{xy}(x,y),$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $f_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)+y(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\\cdot x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, to find $f_{yx}(x,y),$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $f_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)+x(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\\cdot y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043864/example-3129.json",
          "file_sha256": "4a7f52ebf1a72d045ed6188de3e056212d29ae5a404e5140e459d9af5e3ec844"
        }
